  "notes": "Erick Sermon's P-funk flips, and the research separates this from its EPMD sibling on one axis: room. \"Def Squad drums are wide and roomy rather than dry\" is the opposite of every 12-bit entry here, so `velocityVar` is 0.22 and `quantizeStrength` 0.72 — the loosest pair in this batch apart from ODB — over a swung 8th hat at 0.6. The other half is the same funk-bass claim EPMD carries but stated one notch weaker: \"filtered funk bass riff with real syncopation, mixed loud; the groove's melodic anchor\", so `followRootsProb` sits at 0.3 rather than EPMD's 0.25 and the melody keeps a real answer phrase above it. \"Low note density, high groove\" sets `melody.densityPerBar` at 2–5; `timbreHint` is the talkbox/synth-lead answer the research names. ⛔ `chords.impliedByRiff` is a description of \"static I7/i7\", and its variety comes from `chordDurationBeats` and three `harmonicRhythm` values rather than from progressions the records do not play; the `I–IV` family is INFERRED at the lowest weight so the bass has a second reachable root. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it medium-high — the producer's own account is documented but the numbers are not. The inferred parameters are the swing amount and the tambourine density. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 48 to 45 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 46 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 3.",
